---
import BaseLayout from "../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { MAIN_SITE_URL } from "../../../shared/config/config";

const OPERATOR_INFO = "{OPERATOR-INFO}";
const OPERATOR_EMAIL = "{OPERATOR-EMAIL}";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: "Editorial Policy",
  url: MAIN_SITE_URL + "/editorial-policy/",
  description: "How content is produced, labeled, de-identified, and corrected.",
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "name": "Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform", "url": "https://selfcontrolatlas.com" },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle="Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform"
  siteUrl={MAIN_SITE_URL}
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How content is produced, labeled, de-identified, and corrected."
  canonical="/editorial-policy/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Editorial Policy", url: "/editorial-policy/" }]}
>
<h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
<p>This policy explains how content on SelfControlAtlas is produced, labeled, de-identified,
and corrected. Transparency is a core value: readers should be able to see how a page came
to be and how much to trust it.</p>
<p><strong>Last updated: 2026-10-23</strong></p>

<h2>1. Purpose and scope</h2>
<p>This policy governs all content on the site, including experience records, community
patterns, problem answers, strategy pages, research summaries, and the knowledge base. It
applies to every editor and contributor, and to every page, regardless of when it was
published.</p>
<p>The purpose of this policy is to make the site's editorial process transparent, so
readers can judge content appropriately. A recovery resource should not present itself as
more authoritative than it is.</p>

<h2>2. Content layers and labeling</h2>
<p>Every piece of content is assigned to one of the four layers described in the
<a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>, and is labeled accordingly:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Experiences</strong> — de-identified first-person accounts.</li>
  <li><strong>Patterns</strong> — recurring observations aggregated across experiences.</li>
  <li><strong>Problems</strong> — common questions answered with reference to evidence.</li>
  <li><strong>Evidence</strong> — research summaries tied to published sources.</li>
</ol>
<p>A page never presents a pattern as evidence, or an experience as a fact. The layer is
stated on the page, and readers can always tell which kind of content they are reading.</p>

<h2>3. No fabricated authority</h2>
<p>The site does not claim authority it does not have:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Content is <strong>not authored or reviewed by named medical or mental-health professionals</strong>, and we do not claim that it is.</li>
  <li>We do not invent expert credentials, author bios, or advisory boards.</li>
  <li>We do not fabricate statistics, studies, or sources.</li>
  <li>We do not present community anecdotes as scientific findings.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where a clinical matter is involved, content directs readers to qualified professionals.</p>

<h2>4. De-identification rules</h2>
<p>Community-derived content is de-identified before publication. The following are
<strong>always removed or generalized</strong>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Usernames, real names, and aliases that could identify a person.</li>
  <li>Contact details (email, phone, social handles).</li>
  <li>Specific locations (reduced to region or removed).</li>
  <li>Dates that could identify a person or event.</li>
  <li>Any detail that, in combination, could re-identify an individual.</li>
</ul>
<p>The goal is that no reader can identify the person behind an experience, and no personal
information about community members is published. De-identification is performed by the
editors, and reviewed before publication; it is not left to automation alone.</p>

<h2>5. Evidence standards</h2>
<p>Research summaries cite their sources where possible and follow the evidence tiers in the
<a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>. Editors:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Prefer official classifications (e.g., WHO/ICD-11), systematic reviews, and meta-analyses.</li>
  <li>Label weaker sources (case reports, opinion, community consensus) as such.</li>
  <li>State uncertainty honestly when evidence is mixed or absent.</li>
  <li>Do not cherry-pick sources to support a predetermined conclusion.</li>
  <li>Separate evidence from community experience in every claim.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Accuracy and correction</h2>
<p>Editors aim for accuracy, but errors occur. When an error is identified:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Factual errors are corrected promptly.</li>
  <li>The current version of a page is authoritative.</li>
  <li>Corrections may be noted on the page where material.</li>
</ul>
<p>Readers can report errors via the <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page. Corrections are
handled without requiring the reporter to provide personal information.</p>

<h2>7. Community data and consent</h2>
<p>The site draws patterns and experiences from community discussions. The site:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Does not republish identifiable personal expression.</li>
  <li>Does not claim individual consent to republish; instead it de-identifies so that no individual's words are reproduced.</li>
  <li>Treats community material as a source of aggregate observations, not as content to be scraped and reposted.</li>
</ul>
<p>This approach respects the people behind the experiences while still allowing recurring
patterns to be described.</p>

<h2>8. Separation of content and commerce</h2>
<p>There is no advertising, sponsorship, or paid content on the site (see the
<a href="/advertising-disclosure/">Advertising Disclosure</a>). No third party influences
editorial decisions, and no content is paid for. Editorial independence is part of the
site's trust model.</p>

<h2>9. Review and authorship</h2>
<p>Content is produced and reviewed by the project's editors, who are not medical
professionals and do not present themselves as such. The site is maintained by
<strong>{OPERATOR_INFO}</strong>. For corrections, questions, or copyright matters, contact
<strong>{OPERATOR_EMAIL}</strong>.</p>

<h2>10. Handling of sensitive topics</h2>
<p>The site covers sensitive topics — pornography, compulsive sexual behavior, mental health,
and self-harm. Editors follow specific rules for these:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Crisis and professional-help signposts are included wherever a page touches on distress or self-harm.</li>
  <li>Content is written to inform, not to shame or moralize.</li>
  <li>Content is not written to exploit, shock, or sensationalize.</li>
  <li>No content encourages self-harm, and any mention of self-harm is paired with a link to crisis resources.</li>
</ul>

<h2>11. Reviewer independence</h2>
<p>Editors make decisions based on the evidence and the published methodology, not on
external pressure. No advertiser, sponsor, or outside party reviews, approves, or alters
content. The site has no commercial stakeholders.</p>

<h2>12. Corrections log</h2>
<p>Where a correction is material, the site may note it. Routine typographical fixes are made
silently. The current version of any page is authoritative, and readers are encouraged to
report anything that looks wrong via the <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page.</p>

<h2>13. Changes to this policy</h2>
<p>This policy may be updated. The current version is always on this page, with the "Last
updated" date above revised accordingly. Material changes will be reflected in the policy's
content.</p>

<p><em>Last updated: 2026-10-23.</em></p>
</BaseLayout>
